  "riskClass": "low",
  "title": "api-gateway Redis token blacklist fail-open behavior allows revoked tokens through when Redis is down",
  "description": "R6 deep audit finding. Dimension: error_handling. Severity: P3. Source SHA: bbd487a3. Scope: R6_DEEP_AUDIT_WAVE. The jwtAuth middleware's Redis blacklist check (lines 220-237) catches errors and fails open: `catch (blacklistError) { console.warn('[T-184] Redis blacklist check failed (failing open):', blacklistError); }`. The comment at line 234-235 says 'downstream services still check DB-based revocation as a fallback'. However, this DB-based fallback is NOT implemented in any downstream service routes — the auth-service's refresh token revocation only blocks new access token generation, it doesn't validate existing access tokens on every request. This means if Redis goes down, ALL previously revoked tokens become valid again for the remainder of their expiry window (up to 24 hours for supplier tokens).",
